00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:07.080
Speaker 2: All right, it is quite philosophical, and it may make you feel a bit hazy, but let's get into it.

00:07:07.080 --> 00:07:08.240
Speaker 2: Now, there's a core claim.

00:07:08.850 --> 00:07:15.330
Speaker 2: The claim of the ontological argument is this, that God is the greatest possible being.

00:07:16.450 --> 00:07:28.050
Speaker 2: God is the greatest possible being, and a greater possible being cannot exist, or the greatest possible being cannot exist only in the imagination.

00:07:29.730 --> 00:07:32.290
Speaker 2: Therefore God must exist in reality.

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:33.410
Speaker 2: I'll explain.

00:07:33.810 --> 00:07:48.970
Speaker 2: He's saying if you can think of a being that is all powerful, if you can think of a being that has all might in all the world, then that being cannot exist only in your imagination, that being actually exists.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:53.930
Speaker 2: I think it's pushing the limits of your imagination.

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:57.450
Speaker 2: If you can imagine that such a thing exists, then it exists.

00:07:57.610 --> 00:07:59.290
Speaker 2: Yeah, that's what he's trying to say.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:00.810
Speaker 2: So if you were to break it down.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:02.610
Speaker 2: Well, the history of it.

00:08:02.610 --> 00:08:07.536
Speaker 2: First, this was started in the year 1078.

00:08:07.864 --> 00:08:13.900
Speaker 2: 1078, By one of the church fathers, Anselm of Canterbury.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:15.420
Speaker 2: Okay.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:17.420
Speaker 2: And so there's a formal structure to this.

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.420
