00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:36.900
Speaker 2: That's God.

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:43.780
Speaker 2: All power or all powerful, all glorious, you know, all knowing, all loving and all that.

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:45.580
Speaker 2: So the second premise.

00:08:45.580 --> 00:08:52.740
Speaker 2: Now, a being that exists in reality is greater than one that exists only in the imagination.

00:08:54.100 --> 00:08:54.660
Speaker 2: Okay?

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:59.910
Speaker 2: A being that exists in reality is greater than one that exists only in the imagination.

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:07.830
Speaker 2: And then the third premise, if God existed only in the imagination, then we could imagine something greater.

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:09.270
Speaker 2: You see what he's trying to say here?

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:11.990
Speaker 2: So think about the most powerful being.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:22.830
Speaker 2: He's saying that if you can think of it, then there is something greater in reality because it's saying, in a sense that your mind cannot really reach the, you know, finiteness.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:23.150
Speaker 2: You.

00:09:23.150 --> 00:09:25.090
Speaker 2: You can't get to the end of the.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:26.200
Speaker 2: That sort of imagination.

00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:31.480
Speaker 2: So if you think of all power, whatever, there is something greater, there is a next step.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:39.800
Speaker 2: So if you can ever think of an imagination of God in your mind, then that means there is a God that is greater than what your mind can fathom.

00:09:39.880 --> 00:09:41.800
Speaker 2: Yeah, that's the argument.

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:44.520
